From 2ceb65715bbb6d10cf6f28525c0a24dd4cb2042e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: PathOps <noreply@pathops.dev>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 23:49:54 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 6/6] registry: short-lived registry tokens for Application
 build workloads

PathOps Decision 0056: an Application's build ServiceAccount
(<app>-build in <app>-ci) exchanges its projected Kubernetes token at Dex
(RFC 8693 through the Kubernetes workload connector, requested_token_type
urn:pathops:params:oauth:token-type:registry) for a registry token: a JWT
signed with a dedicated Ed25519 key (never Dex's OIDC keys), with its own
issuer and audience, at most 5 minutes, carrying the Docker Distribution
"access" claim for exactly <prefix><application id> with pull and push.

The Application id comes from the Kubernetes API, read with Dex's own
ServiceAccount: the workload's Namespace and ServiceAccount must carry the
same pathops.io/application-id label, the Namespace pathops.io/ci=true and
the ServiceAccount pathops.io/capability=build-and-push. Only the
configured clients and connector may ask, only with an ID token. Any other
identity is refused (access_denied), with the reason logged only.

Configuration (opt-in; without it nothing changes):

  registryTokens:
    clients: [zot]
    connector: k8s-cell
    issuer: https://dex.example.org/registry
    audience: zot-registry
    signingKeyFile: /etc/dex/registry/registry.key
    keyID: registry-1
    validFor: 5m
    repositoryPrefix: apps/
